     <p>Brian Epstein’s compact eighty-seven-page Cambridge Element <em>Social Ontology</em> provides an impressive, ecumenical overview of the field of social ontology. Epstein characterizes social ontology as concerned, in the first instance, with addressing the ‘What is X?’ question, with ‘X’ replaced by a term about a social object, kind, category, etc. For example, what is a corporation, what is race, what are group attitudes, what is law? No mere summary, the book’s goal is to provide a general, flexible framework for thinking about social kinds. How are inquiries characteristically structured? How can theories be compared? What are the various goals theorists of the social have (for example, ontological reduction vs. critique and amelioration)? What order can we bring to understanding how theories are constructed, and what aspects of...
